{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"a000000000000001","type":"text","x":-100,"y":-820,"width":6600,"height":380,"color":"5","text":"## Memory Oligopoly Map\n\n**DRAM = mature 3-player triopoly · NAND = consolidating 5-player oligopoly (+1 Chinese disruptor)**\n\nDRAM consolidated 9→3 across 2007-2013 via Elpida BK (2012) → Micron acquired (2013). NAND consolidated 12→5+1 across 1990s-2025 via Toshiba scandal (2016 → Kioxia), Intel NAND sale (2021 → SK Hynix/Solidigm), WDC spin (Feb 2025 → SanDisk pure-play). YMTC PCIe 5.0 launch Mar 2026 puts a confirmed consumer-price disruptor on Entity List perimeter.\n\n**Key 2026 dynamics**: SK Hynix HBM share 57% Q1 2026 (peak 62%, ceding to Samsung HBM4 qualification at Nvidia 'best scores' → Rubin 70/30 split) · Samsung NAND V10 (420-430L Oct 2026) ships with **YMTC hybrid-bonding IP** — first external technology dependency in Samsung memory history · Kioxia+SanDisk Flash Ventures JV cost leader at $0.060-0.065/GB · Micron NAND sub-scale cost loser at $0.072-0.077/GB · HBF (hybrid-bonded flash) emerges as third memory tier with OCP standardization Feb 2026.\n\n**Triopoly pricing-discipline test**: SK Hynix posted **72% Q1 2026 op margin — memory-industry record**. Bull case = evergreen 25x mid-cycle re-rating (60% base / 25% partial / 15% full reset per sector note). Diagnostic = two consecutive Q with HBM ASP flat-rising while one vendor's share falls 5+pp (never observed in DRAM history; first test window Q3-Q4 2026)."},
		{"id":"b000000000000001","type":"group","x":-100,"y":-420,"width":6600,"height":340,"label":"Industry Consolidation Timeline — DRAM 9→3 (1991-2013) · NAND 12→6 (1990s-2025)","color":"3"},
		{"id":"a000000000000010","type":"text","x":-60,"y":-380,"width":1290,"height":280,"text":"### 1980s-1991\n**Japanese conquest**\n\nHitachi, NEC, Toshiba, Mitsubishi, Fujitsu peak ~75% DRAM share 1988. Intel exits DRAM 1985 (Andy Grove: 'we don't have a memory company'). 1986 US-Japan Trade Agreement establishes price floor — ironically benefits Korean entrants."},
		{"id":"a000000000000011","type":"text","x":1250,"y":-380,"width":1290,"height":280,"text":"### 1991-2007\n**Korean ascent + DOJ cartel**\n\nSamsung passes every Japanese leader via countercyclical investment. Hyundai → Hynix → SK Hynix (2011). DOJ DRAM cartel investigation 2002-07 → **$731M fines** (Samsung $300M, Hynix $185M, Infineon $160M, Micron coop). Did not break oligopoly — disciplined it."},
		{"id":"a000000000000012","type":"text","x":2560,"y":-380,"width":1290,"height":280,"text":"### 2008-2013\n**Forced consolidation → triopoly forms**\n\n2008-09 Qimonda BK ($3B losses). 2012 Elpida BK ($5.5B liabilities — largest JP corporate failure since JAL). 2013 **Micron acquires Elpida ¥200B (~free)** → modern DRAM triopoly (Samsung, SK Hynix, Micron). NAND: 2016 Toshiba scandal → Kioxia (Bain consortium)."},
		{"id":"a000000000000013","type":"text","x":3870,"y":-380,"width":1290,"height":280,"text":"### 2018-2024\n**NAND consolidation continues**\n\n2018 NAND down-cycle 50%+ price drop. **2021 SK Hynix acquires Intel NAND $9B → Solidigm**. 2022 YMTC Entity List + >128L tool export ban. 2023-24 NAND/DRAM trough — Samsung memory losses; **20-30% wafer-start cuts** seed 2025-27 supply deficit."},
		{"id":"a000000000000014","type":"text","x":5180,"y":-380,"width":1290,"height":280,"text":"### 2025-2026\n**Pure-plays + HBM era**\n\n**Feb 2025: WDC → SanDisk spin-off** (first NAND pure-play since Spansion; +3,271% in 14mo). **Dec 2025: Kioxia IPO** ¥1,455 → ¥35,950 by Apr 2026 (~$107B mcap). Aug 2025: SanDisk+SK Hynix **HBF MOU**. Feb 2026: OCP HBF standardization. Apr 2026: SanDisk pilot accelerated 6mo."},
		{"id":"b000000000000002","type":"group","x":-100,"y":80,"width":6600,"height":840,"label":"DRAM Triopoly (~$120-130B 2026E TAM; HBM ~$35-40B sub-segment)","color":"4"},
		{"id":"a000000000000020","type":"text","x":-60,"y":140,"width":2150,"height":80,"color":"4","text":"## Samsung Semiconductor — Memory Division\n*~33% DRAM share Q1 2026 (lost #1 to SK Hynix in 2024 after 24 of prior 25 years)*"},
		{"id":"a000000000000021","type":"text","x":-60,"y":230,"width":2150,"height":660,"text":"### Position\n- **DRAM share**: ~33% (Q1 2026) — vertical integration\n- **HBM share**: ~30-35% (HBM3E qualification miss at Nvidia 2024 was the inflection)\n- **Rubin SKU allocation**: ~30% (vs SK Hynix 70%) — Samsung HBM4 12-Hi reportedly scored 'best' at Nvidia qual\n- **Memory CY26 capex**: ~$20B (+11% YoY)\n- **DRAM node**: 1c-nm leadership\n- **Customer base**: broad (Apple, Google, NVDA partial, mobile)\n\n### Strategic posture\n- $73B 2026 Samsung Electronics capex — disciplined add or seed of overshoot? (sector-note key question)\n- HBM3E 12-Hi recovery + HBM4 qual = the 2026 narrative\n- **Memory countercyclical doctrine** invented here (1990s-2000s) — built into downturns to bankrupt weaker peers\n\n### Diagnostic\nWhether Q1 2026 DDR5-profitability-surpassing-HBM3E inversion signals return to scale dominance (Samsung favored) or one-quarter aberration before HBM4 reasserts SK Hynix\n\n**No vault thesis** — exposure via [[Sectors/DRAM & HBM Memory]] sector frame"},
		{"id":"a000000000000030","type":"text","x":2150,"y":140,"width":2200,"height":80,"color":"4","text":"## SK Hynix — The HBM Triopoly Leader\n*~37% DRAM share · **HBM 57% share Q1 2026** · **72% Q1 op margin — memory-industry record***"},
		{"id":"a000000000000031","type":"file","file":"Theses/000660 - SK Hynix.md","x":2150,"y":230,"width":2200,"height":420,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"a000000000000032","type":"text","x":2150,"y":660,"width":2200,"height":230,"text":"### HBM dominance\n- **HBM peak 62% (HBM3E era)** → 57% Q1 2026 → forecast 48-52% by HBM4E (2027-28)\n- **Sole-source HBM3 at NVDA H100 2022** — the inflection that built today's pricing power\n- HBM4 12-Hi: 70% Rubin allocation; HBM5 hybrid-bonding-ready (Kinex first mass-prod order Mar 2026)\n\n### Korean discount opportunity\nSK Hynix **5-8x P/E** vs Micron 11-14x P/E on equivalent 2026E. Sell-side reads as fixed; precedents (2016-17 KOSPI re-rate, 2024 Kioxia IPO re-rate) argue otherwise"},
		{"id":"a000000000000040","type":"text","x":4350,"y":140,"width":2150,"height":80,"color":"4","text":"## Micron — The US-Aligned #3\n*~24% DRAM share (post-Elpida 2013) · HBM3E entered late at Nvidia · CHIPS Act anchor*"},
		{"id":"a000000000000041","type":"text","x":4350,"y":230,"width":2150,"height":660,"text":"### Position\n- **DRAM share**: ~24% — smallest of triopoly but US-aligned premium\n- **HBM share**: ~10-15% (HBM3E qualified at NVDA late 2024; HBM4E custom base die via TSMC for 2027-28)\n- **Memory CY26 capex**: $13.5B (+23% YoY)\n- **NAND**: also #5 in NAND — sub-scale, cost loser at $0.072-0.077/GB\n- **CHIPS Act**: Boise/Syracuse fab anchor; political moat against China indigenization\n\n### Strategic posture\n- HBM = the lever; can Micron grow HBM share from ~12% → 25%?\n- NAND is sub-scale — capex allocation increasingly HBM-tilted (NAND R&D residual)\n- DDR5 transition leadership\n\n### Why it matters\n**Micron is the variance-reduction trade vs Korean discount** — same triopoly economics, US listing, no chaebol governance overhang. Trades at 11-14x vs SK Hynix 5-8x.\n\n**No vault thesis** — exposure via [[Sectors/DRAM & HBM Memory]]"},
		{"id":"b000000000000003","type":"group","x":-100,"y":940,"width":6600,"height":560,"label":"★ HBM Sub-Monopoly Within DRAM — AI-Driven, ~$35-40B 2026E TAM, ~95% to Accelerators","color":"5"},
		{"id":"a000000000000050","type":"text","x":-60,"y":1000,"width":2150,"height":480,"color":"5","text":"## HBM as Architecture-Driven Pricing Power\n\nHBM transformed from **<$3B/yr niche pre-ChatGPT (Nov 2022)** to ~$35-40B 2026E TAM — the structural reset of memory economics.\n\n**SK Hynix dominance origin**: HBM1 ratified JEDEC Oct 2013 as joint AMD/SK Hynix spec. AMD Fiji 2015 = first commercial HBM. Tesla P100 2016 = first NVDA volume. **SK Hynix sole-source on HBM3 at H100 launch 2022** is the moat that compounds today.\n\n**Q1 2026 inversion** (sector note key question #2):\nDDR5 profitability surpassed HBM3E for first time since 2024. Signal of scale-leader return (Samsung) or one-Q aberration before HBM4 reasserts SK Hynix? Determines whether SK Hynix's #1 position is cyclical or structural."},
		{"id":"a000000000000051","type":"text","x":2150,"y":1000,"width":2200,"height":480,"text":"### HBM Share Trajectory\n\n| Gen | Year | SK Hynix | Samsung | Micron |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HBM1 | 2013 | >90% | — | — |\n| HBM2 | 2016 | ~70% | enters | — |\n| HBM2E | 2020 | ~50% | catches | — |\n| HBM3 | 2022 | ~50%↗ | trails | — |\n| HBM3E | 2024 | **62%** | qual miss | enters |\n| HBM4 12-Hi | 2026 | **57%** | qual pass | gains |\n| HBM4E 16-Hi | 2027-28 | 48-52% | recovery | custom base die |\n\n### Kill mechanism on HBM incumbent\n**Not die yield — integration boundary failures.** Samsung 2024 miss was thermal/noise at stack after MR-MUF, not individual die. SK Hynix's HBM5 risk = 20-Hi hybrid-bonding equipment maturity.\n\n**Diagnostic** for vendor shift: at which integration step does next failure happen, which vendor's tooling/process IP is positioned for it."},
		{"id":"a000000000000052","type":"text","x":4350,"y":1000,"width":2150,"height":480,"text":"### HBM Value Chain → Vault Cross-Refs\n\n**Customer demand pull**:\n- [[Theses/NVDA - Nvidia]] — 60%+ HBM offtake\n- [[Theses/AMD - Advanced Micro Devices]] — MI300/MI400 series\n- Hyperscaler custom silicon ([[Theses/AVGO - Broadcom]] TPU for Google, [[Theses/MRVL - Marvell Technology]] Trainium fabric)\n\n**Equipment supply** (HBM-specific):\n- [[Theses/LRCX - Lam Research]] — TSV etch, ECD plating\n- [[Theses/AMAT - Applied Materials]] — CMP, bond prep, AGS service\n- [[Theses/KLA - KLA Corporation]] — bump/TSV inspection (3x intensity in adv packaging)\n- [[Theses/BESI - BE Semiconductor Industries]] — D2W hybrid bonding for HBM5 (HBM4 deferred to microbump/MR-MUF per JEDEC 900µm relaxation Apr 2026)\n- [[Theses/FORM - FormFactor]] — HBM probe cards (2-3x test intensity vs DRAM)\n\n**Substrates**: [[Theses/2802 - Ajinomoto]] ABF substrates"},
		{"id":"b000000000000004","type":"group","x":-100,"y":1540,"width":7700,"height":1020,"label":"NAND 5-Player Oligopoly + YMTC (China Disruptor) — ~$60-70B 2026E TAM","color":"2"},
		{"id":"a000000000000060","type":"text","x":-60,"y":1600,"width":1240,"height":80,"color":"4","text":"## Samsung NAND\n*#1 ~31% share*"},
		{"id":"a000000000000061","type":"text","x":-60,"y":1690,"width":1240,"height":840,"text":"### Position\n- **Share**: ~31% (eroding)\n- **Node**: V10 (420-430L) **Oct 2026** — first ship with **YMTC hybrid-bonding IP**\n- **Architecture**: V-NAND (charge-trap)\n- **NAND capex CY26**: flat ~$2B (capital starvation)\n\n### Moat erosion\n**First external technology dependency in Samsung memory history** — V10 hybrid-bonding licensed from YMTC. Historical 18-month 3D lead (V-NAND 2013) is gone.\n\n### Cost/bit\n~$0.067-0.072/GB (mid-tier)\n\n### Customer base\nMobile, datacenter SSD, vertical-integrated to Samsung phones/SSDs.\n\n*No vault thesis*"},
		{"id":"a000000000000070","type":"text","x":1190,"y":1600,"width":1240,"height":80,"color":"4","text":"## SK Hynix + Solidigm\n*#2 ~22% combined*"},
		{"id":"a000000000000071","type":"text","x":1190,"y":1690,"width":1240,"height":840,"text":"### Position\n- **Share**: ~22% (SK Hynix native + Solidigm acquired from Intel 2021 for **$9B**)\n- **Node**: 321L QLC in mass-production\n- **QLC dominance**: **51% QLC market share** — enterprise SSD leadership\n- **244TB SSD on track** — largest single SSD on market\n\n### Strategic flex\n- SK Hynix HBM cash funds Solidigm QLC R&D\n- QLC = the high-capacity, read-heavy AI inference storage tier\n- HBF MOU partner with SanDisk (Aug 2025)\n\n### Cost/bit\n~$0.064-0.069/GB\n\n*Exposure via [[Theses/000660 - SK Hynix]]* — same entity"},
		{"id":"a000000000000080","type":"text","x":2440,"y":1600,"width":1240,"height":80,"color":"4","text":"## Kioxia\n*#3 ~20% share*"},
		{"id":"a000000000000081","type":"file","file":"Theses/285A - Kioxia.md","x":2440,"y":1690,"width":1240,"height":420,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"a000000000000082","type":"text","x":2440,"y":2120,"width":1240,"height":410,"text":"### Differentiation\n- **BiCS10 (332L)** expedited to 2026 — **+59% density to 29 Gb/mm²**, 4µs latency, -29% power\n- **CBA architecture** quantifiably superior\n- **Flash Ventures JV with SanDisk** — shared capex, lowest cost/bit (~$0.060-0.065/GB)\n- 2026 NAND #3, but **lowest cost-per-bit globally** when combined with JV partner\n\n### Catalysts\n- BiCS10 ramp 2026\n- 400L cryogenic etch HVM 2026 (TEL equipment)\n- Dec 2025 IPO ¥1,455 → ¥35,950 by Apr 2026 (re-rate continuing)"},
		{"id":"a000000000000090","type":"text","x":3690,"y":1600,"width":1240,"height":80,"color":"4","text":"## SanDisk\n*#4 ~13% share*"},
		{"id":"a000000000000091","type":"file","file":"Theses/SNDK - SanDisk.md","x":3690,"y":1690,"width":1240,"height":420,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"a000000000000092","type":"text","x":3690,"y":2120,"width":1240,"height":410,"text":"### The HBF inventor\n- **HBF (Hybrid-Bonded Flash)** invented Aug 2025; OCP standardization Feb 2026\n- **Pilot line accelerated 6 months to H2 2026** (Japan)\n- WDC spin Feb 2025 = first NAND pure-play since Spansion\n- +3,271% stock in 14 months post-IPO\n\n### Flash Ventures JV\nShared capex with Kioxia = cost-parity at ~$0.060-0.065/GB. The JV is the structural moat.\n\n### HBF as 3rd memory tier\nSimulation: 2.69x perf/watt, 18.8x batch, 32→2 GPU reduction vs HBM-only stack"},
		{"id":"a0000000000000a0","type":"text","x":4940,"y":1600,"width":1240,"height":80,"color":"4","text":"## Micron NAND\n*#5 ~9% share*"},
		{"id":"a0000000000000a1","type":"text","x":4940,"y":1690,"width":1240,"height":840,"text":"### Position\n- **Share**: ~9% — sub-scale, cost loser\n- **Cost/bit**: $0.072-0.077/GB (worst of major-5)\n- **Differentiator**: PCIe 6.0 first-mover (28 GB/s); **245TB 6600 ION SSD** H1 2026 qual\n- **NAND capex CY26**: lean — HBM-prioritized\n\n### Strategic dilemma\nMicron is structurally sub-scale in NAND. HBM cash should fund DRAM/HBM, not NAND. Q: divest NAND? Likely answer: no — IO-speed moat + CHIPS Act anchor preserve strategic value.\n\n### IO-speed moat\n- 245TB 6600 ION = largest commercial SSD\n- PCIe 6.0 leadership = AI training storage tier\n\n*No direct vault thesis*"},
		{"id":"a0000000000000b0","type":"text","x":6190,"y":1600,"width":1370,"height":80,"color":"1","text":"## YMTC (China)\n*~6% share + growing · Entity List*"},
		{"id":"a0000000000000b1","type":"text","x":6190,"y":1690,"width":1370,"height":840,"text":"### The consumer-price disruptor\n- **PC550 launched Mar 2026** — first Chinese PCIe 5.0 SSD, Xtacking 4.0\n- 232L NAND in volume\n- **Phase III fast-tracked to H2 2026** (+1yr ahead)\n- 2 additional fabs planned (~400K WSPM total at full scale)\n- **30-35% Cost/TB discount** on consumer; ~75-80% yield (vs incumbents 85-92%)\n- Half the endurance, zero enterprise penetration\n\n### Sanctions perimeter\n- Entity List 2022\n- >128L tool export ban\n- Pentagon briefly removed from 1260H list Feb 2026 (then withdrawn) — **Entity List remains binding**\n\n### Enterprise gap **WIDENING**\n- Western: 245TB PCIe 5.0\n- YMTC: 15.36TB PCIe 4.0\n- **16x gap** at enterprise tier\n\n### Strategic threat\n**Samsung V10 ships with YMTC hybrid-bonding IP** — first non-Korean tech dependency in Samsung memory. Signal of YMTC IP quality, not just price disruption."},
		{"id":"b000000000000005","type":"group","x":-100,"y":2600,"width":7700,"height":420,"label":"★ HBF (Hybrid-Bonded Flash) — Emerging Third Memory Tier","color":"5"},
		{"id":"a0000000000000c0","type":"text","x":-60,"y":2660,"width":2530,"height":320,"color":"5","text":"## HBF: A New Memory Tier Between HBM and SSD\n\n**Architecture**: NAND dies hybrid-bonded into stack adjacent to GPU/accelerator. Read-intensive AI inference workload tier. Fills the bandwidth-capacity gap between HBM ($/GB high, capacity low) and SSD ($/GB low, latency high).\n\n**Simulation results** (SK Hynix H3, 8x HBM3E + 8x HBF + B200 GPU):\n- **2.69x perf/watt**\n- **18.8x batch size**\n- **32 → 2 GPU reduction** for same workload\n\n**Why this matters for the oligopoly**: HBF re-orders NAND vendor value. Vendors with hybrid-bonding capability (Kioxia, SanDisk via Flash Ventures JV; SK Hynix via 3D integration) become next-tier suppliers. Micron's PCIe-6 moat doesn't translate. Samsung's V-NAND advantage diminishes."},
		{"id":"a0000000000000c1","type":"text","x":2510,"y":2660,"width":2530,"height":320,"color":"5","text":"## HBF De-Risking Signals (5)\n\n1. **SanDisk pilot accelerated 6mo to H2 2026** (Japan-based)\n2. **SK Hynix simulation data published** (2.69x perf/watt, 32→2 GPU)\n3. **Samsung researching since early 2020s + patent filings** (passive but engaged)\n4. **Kioxia GP Series — 10M+ IOPS flash module** announced at GTC 2026\n5. **OCP HBF standardization workstream launched Feb 2026** (industry-wide spec)\n\n## Gen-1 limits\n- Write endurance ~100K cycles → read-intensive workloads only\n- NVDA Rubin (H2 2026) does **not** natively support HBF\n- First GPU integration likely **late 2027 / early 2028**\n\n## Vs Optane\nHBF differs structurally — NAND-based scalability, multi-vendor OCP standard, concrete $/inference ROI. Optane failed on proprietary single-vendor + vague value prop."},
		{"id":"a0000000000000c2","type":"text","x":5080,"y":2660,"width":2480,"height":320,"color":"5","text":"## HBF Winners by Vendor\n\n**Tier 1 (inventors)**:\n- **SanDisk + Kioxia (Flash Ventures JV)** — invented; lowest cost\n- **SK Hynix** — simulation partner; 3D integration capability\n\n**Tier 2 (fast-follower)**:\n- Samsung — researching since early 2020s; patent base\n\n**Tier 3 (lagging)**:\n- Micron — no announced HBF program; PCIe-6 focus continues\n- YMTC — zero hybrid-bonding capability; consumer-only\n\n## Equipment beneficiaries\n- **BESI** — D2W hybrid bonding scales beyond HBM\n- **AMAT + BESI Kinex JV** — D2W expansion\n- **TEL** — cryogenic etch for high-layer count\n\n*Cross-ref [[Sectors/NAND Memory & Storage]] HBF research notes*"},
		{"id":"b000000000000006","type":"group","x":-100,"y":3060,"width":7700,"height":500,"label":"Cycle Dynamics + Threat Vectors + Demand Drivers","color":"2"},
		{"id":"a0000000000000d0","type":"text","x":-60,"y":3120,"width":2530,"height":400,"text":"## Pricing Discipline — Has the Cycle Broken?\n\n**Historical reflexive loop** (every NAND cycle 2008-2023):\nStage 1 capex → Stage 2 supply 12-24mo → Stage 3 ASP crash → Stage 4 margin collapse → Stage 1 again\n\n**Current cycle is FIRST WHERE STAGE-4 PROFITS ARE NOT BEING RECYCLED into Stage-1 capex**:\n- Samsung NAND capex **flat at ~$2B**\n- No greenfield NAND fab until Micron Fab 10B (H2 **2028**)\n- Bit supply growth 15-17% YoY vs demand 20-22% — **deficit widening**\n- Q2 2026 contract ASP: spot +70-75% QoQ, LTA +30-40% QoQ\n\n**Triopoly evergreen-reset hypothesis** (DRAM):\n- 60% base / 25% partial / 15% full re-rate to 25x mid-cycle\n- SK Hynix full-reset upside: **+210-310%**\n- Samsung full-reset: +50-110%\n- Micron full-reset: +90-130%\n- Kioxia full-reset: +120%\n- Diagnostic: 2 consecutive Q with HBM ASP flat-rising + 1 vendor share -5pp (never observed)"},
		{"id":"a0000000000000d1","type":"text","x":2510,"y":3120,"width":2530,"height":400,"color":"1","text":"## Threat Vectors (DRAM + NAND)\n\n### China indigenization\n- **CXMT (DRAM)** — DDR4/LPDDR5 in production; target ~10% share 2027; **Entity List 2024**\n- **YMTC (NAND)** — 232L Xtacking 4.0; consumer-price disruptor; 16x enterprise gap (widening)\n- **Samsung V10 with YMTC HB IP** — first cross-border memory tech dependency since the 1980s\n\n### Triopoly break conditions\n- 4th entrant breaking discipline (unlikely — no path for CXMT to 15%+ in DRAM)\n- Samsung capex undisciplined (sector-note question — $73B 2026 capex)\n- AI demand collapse below +30-40% YoY (DeepSeek-style inference efficiency)\n\n### Korean discount risk\nSK Hynix 5-8x P/E gap to Micron — persistent governance discount, or re-rating setup? Precedents (2016-17 KOSPI, 2024 Kioxia IPO) argue for re-rate\n\n### Export controls\n- US/Dutch/Japan trilateral on >128L tools (NAND), HBM (DRAM)\n- MATCH Act DUV servicing extension Apr 2026"},
		{"id":"a0000000000000d2","type":"text","x":5080,"y":3120,"width":2480,"height":400,"text":"## Demand Drivers (2026)\n\n### HBM (the AI sub-segment)\n- 60%+ to NVDA (Blackwell + Rubin)\n- Custom base dies: SK Hynix on TSMC 3nm, Samsung 2nm internal, Micron TSMC custom logic\n- Q: half-life of customer lock-in across GPU generations? (sector key question)\n\n### DDR5 mainstream transition\n- ~70% DRAM mix by end-2026\n- Server-led; PC follows\n\n### Mobile UFS\n- LPDDR5X → LPDDR6 transition\n- AI-on-device (Galaxy S26, iPhone 18 AI)\n\n### Datacenter SSD (QLC)\n- AI inference storage tier\n- SK Hynix/Solidigm 51% QLC share\n- 245TB SSDs (Micron) + 244TB (Solidigm)\n\n### Auto memory\n- AD compute + LPDDR5X cabin\n- Sub-5% of TAM but margin premium\n\n### Capex by hyperscalers\nMemory benefits from $600-750B CY26 hyperscaler capex (sector cross-ref [[Canvas/AI Infrastructure Full Stack]])"},
		{"id":"b000000000000007","type":"group","x":-100,"y":3600,"width":7700,"height":380,"label":"Sector MOC Embeds + Vault Cross-References","color":"5"},
		{"id":"a0000000000000e0","type":"file","file":"Sectors/DRAM & HBM Memory.md","x":-60,"y":3660,"width":2530,"height":300,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"a0000000000000e1","type":"file","file":"Sectors/NAND Memory & Storage.md","x":2510,"y":3660,"width":2530,"height":300,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"a0000000000000e2","type":"text","x":5080,"y":3660,"width":2480,"height":300,"color":"5","text":"## Related Canvases\n\n- [[Canvas/AI Infrastructure Full Stack]] — memory in full demand-pull context (NVDA → SK Hynix HBM)\n- [[Canvas/Semiconductor Capital Equipment Value Chain]] — equipment side: LRCX memory etch, BESI HBM hybrid bonding, TEL cryogenic etch\n- [[Canvas/Pricing Power Matrix]] — DRAM triopoly vs NAND oligopoly placement\n- [[Canvas/Quality vs Valuation Scatter]] — SK Hynix Korean-discount placement (Bargain Compounder)\n\n## Active Theses on Canvas\n- [[Theses/000660 - SK Hynix]] — HBM triopoly leader, conviction MEDIUM\n- [[Theses/285A - Kioxia]] — NAND CBA leader, conviction MEDIUM\n- [[Theses/SNDK - SanDisk]] — HBF inventor, conviction MEDIUM\n\n## No Vault Thesis\nSamsung (broad conglomerate exposure), Micron (US-listed alternative)"}
	],
